==English==
{{was wotd|2016|January|8}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|rāz}}
** {{IPA|en|/ɹeɪz/|a=RP,GA,CA}}
*** {{audio|en|en-us-raise.ogg|a=California}}
** {{IPA|en|/ɹæɪz/|a=AU,NZ}}
*** {{audio|en|en-au-raze.ogg|a=Queensland}}
* {{homophones|en|raise|rase|rays|rehs|réis|res}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪz|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|rasen}}, {{m|enm|racen}}, {{m|enm|rase|t=to scrape; to shave; to erase; to pull; to strip off; to pluck or tear out; to root out (a tree, etc.); to pull away, snatch; to pull down; to knock down; to rend, tear apart; to pick clean, strip; to cleave, slice; to sever; to lacerate; to pierce; to carve, engrave; to dig; (''figurative'') to expunge, obliterate; to alter}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=rāsen|pos=''v.''(1)|noformat=1|id=MED35943}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|raser}}, {{m|xno|rasere}}, {{m|xno|rasser}}, {{der|en|frm|raser}}, and {{der|en|fro|raser|t=to shave; to touch lightly, graze; to level off (grain, etc.) in a measure; to demolish, tear down; to erase; to polish; to wear down}}, from {{der|en|la-vul|*raso|t=to shave; to scrape; to scratch; to touch lightly, graze}}, from {{der|en|la|rāsus|t=scraped; shaved}}, the {{glossary|perfect}} {{glossary|passive}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|la|rādō|t=to scrape, scratch; to shave; to rub, smooth; to brush along, graze}}.<ref>Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=8012972660|date=March 2025|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref> {{doublet|en|rash}} ([[rash#Etymology 2|etymology 2]] and [[rash#Etymology 7|etymology 7]]).

The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from the verb.<ref>{{R:OED Online|entry=† raze|pos=''n.''{{sup|1}}|noformat=1|id=7060558275|date=July 2023}}</ref>

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## To [[level#Verb|level]] or [[tear down]] (a [[building#Noun|building]], a [[town]], etc.) to the [[ground#Noun|ground]]; to [[demolish]].
##* {{RQ:Froissart Berners Cronycles|year=1970|volume=1|chapter=Howe the Frẽche Kyng Sent a Great Nauy to the See&#11805; ⁊ howe Duyers Townes were Brent in Englande: ⁊ howe the Duke of Burgoyne Tooke Dyuers Castels about Calys|folio=cxcvii|verso=1|column=1|passage=The fortreſſe was '''raſed''' and beaten downe to the erthe&#11805; whiche had coſt moche the makynge therof: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-1|act=II|scene=iii|page=103|column=1|passage=Theſe are his ſubſtance, ſinevves, armes, and ſtrength, / VVith vvhich he yoaketh your rebellious Neckes, / '''Razeth''' your Cities, and ſubuerts your Tovvnes, / And in a moment makes them deſolate.}}
##* {{RQ:King James Version|Psalm|137|7|sig=Hhh2|verso=1|page=717|column=2|passage=Remember, O {{smallcaps|Lord}}, the children of Edom, in the day of Jeruſalem; who ſayd, '''raſe''' it, '''raſe''' it: euen to the foundation thereof.}}
##* {{RQ:Homer Chapman Iliads|book=II|page=22|passage=[N]ovv I find, / Theſe men vvould render thee the ſhame, of all men; nor vvould pay, / Their ovvne vovves to thee, vvhen they tooke, their free and honord vvay, / From Argos hither; that till Troy, vvere by their braue hands '''rac't''', / They vvould not turne home; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Raleigh Mahomet|page=50|passage=For his further ſecuritie he {{quote-gloss|Don Roderigo}} diſarmed his ſubjects; ſuch Caſtles and ſtrengths as hee vvas jealous of vvere '''raced''', {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Herbert Temple|chapter=The Sacrifice|page=21|passage=Some ſaid, that I the Temple to the floore / In three dayes '''raz'd''', and raiſed as before.}}
##* {{RQ:Butler Genuine Remains|volume=I|chapter=Rump-Parliament|page=302|passage=[A]fter they had deſtroyed ''Religion'', they pulled dovvn Churches, (as being then of no Uſe) and '''raſed''' the nobleſt Structures in the Land, to ſell the Materials; {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1755|author=William Green|chapter=A New Version of the Third Chapter of [[w:Book of Habakkuk|Habakkuk]]|title=A New Translation of the Prayer of Habakkuk, the Prayer of Moses, and the CXXXIX [[w:Psalms|Psalm]];{{nb...|with a Commentary on Each. To which are Added Notes Critical and Explanatory.}}|location=Cambridge, Cambridgeshire|publisher={{...|Printed by}} J[oseph] Bentham, printer to the [[w:University of Cambridge|University]]; [s]old by T. Merrill{{nb...|in Cambridge; J. Whiston and B. White in Fleet-Street; and B. Dod in Ave-Mary-Lane, London; J. Fletcher in Oxford; and J. Hildyard at York.}}|section=verse 18|page=10|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=5HVQN6PSK6EC&pg=PA10|oclc=1264947221|passage=Thou vvoundeſt the Head of the Houſe of the VVicked; / Thou '''raſedſt''' the Foundation even to the Rock; / Thou piercedſt thro' vvith thy Scepter the Head of the Villages.}}
##* {{RQ:Grose England and Wales|volume=II|chapter=Leeds Castle, Kent|page=114|passage={{quote-gloss|{{w|Edward I of England}}}} cauſed [[w:Henry de Cobham, 1st Baron Cobham|Henry Cobham]], {{...}} to '''race''' the caſtle that Robert de Crevequer had erected, becauſe Crevequer (that vvas the ovvner of it, and heire to Robert) vvas of the number of the nobles that moved and mainteined vvare againſt him; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Gibbon Roman Empire|volume=II|chapter=Residence of [[w:Julian (emperor)|Julian]] at Antioch—His Successful Expedition against the Persians—{{nb...|Passage of the Tigris—The Retreat and Death of Julian—Election of Jovian—He Saves the Roman Army by a Disgraceful Treaty}}|page=437|passage=The fortifications vvere '''razed''' to the ground; and not a veſtige vvas left, that the city of Maogamalcha had ever existed.}}
##* {{RQ:Prescott Mexico|volume=I|chapter=Decisive Victory—Indian Council—Night Attack—Negotiations with the Enemy—Tlascalan Hero|page=452|passage=He {{quote-gloss|{{w|Hernán Cortés}}}} made the same professions of amity as befo0re, promising oblivion of all past injuries; but, if this proffer was rejected, he would visit their capital as a conqueror, '''raze''' every house in it to the ground, and put every inhabitant to the sword!}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=13 May 2017|author=w:Barney Ronay|title={{w|Antonio Conte}}’s brilliance has turned [[w:Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]]’s pop-up team into champions|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250427205814/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2017/may/13/antonio-conte-chelsea-champions-manager|passage=Just as significant in the long term, Chelsea were also granted permission this season for their new on-site mega-stadium, a 60,000-seat upgrade that will mean the current Stamford Bridge is '''razed''' and replaced by something that looks like a vast alien space yurt made of giant Martian redwood stems.}}
##* {{quote-av|en|date=1 December 2018|author=Drachinifel [pseudonym]|title=Anti-Slavery Patrols – The West Africa Squadron|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20241129115918/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TiSekII0sjw|time=9:45|passage=After his actions were challenged by foreign governments and Parliament initially tried to put a stop to his action, Denman returned home and argued his case with enough force that, by 1848, the Royal Navy was handed active permission and ''encouragement'' to '''raze''' every last slave factory they could find to the ground, and ''full'' authority to stop ''any'' ship, of ''any'' flag, that was thought to be a slaver, with a guarantee with{{sic}} ''no'' censure from the government.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[completely]] [[remove#Verb|remove]] (someone or something), especially from a [[place#Noun|place]], a [[situation#Noun|situation]], etc.; also, to remove from [[existence]]; to [[destroy]], to [[obliterate#Verb|obliterate]].
##: {{synonyms|en|Thesaurus:destroy}}
##* {{RQ:Lyly Euphues|part=2|chapter=To Philautus|folio=71|passage=Yet at the laſt, caſting with my ſelf, that y&#868; heat of thy loue might cleane be '''razed''' with y&#868; coldnes of my letter, I thought it good to commit an inconuenience, that I might preuent a miſchiefe, chuſing rather to cut thée off ſhort by rigour, then to giue thée any iot of hope by ſilence.}}
##* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=i|page=57|passage=VVithout reſpect of ſex, degree or age. / He '''raceth''' all his foes vvith fire and ſvvord.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Titus Andronicus Q1|act=I|scene=I|sig=C2|page=16|passage=Ile find a day to maſſacre them all, / And '''race''' their faction and their familie, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Defoe Devil|chapter=Of the Devil's Second Kingdom, and How He Got Footing in the Renew'd World by His Victory over {{w|Noah}} and His Race|page=156|passage=It is true, the ''[[w:Devil in Christianity|Devil]]'' did not immediately '''raſe''' out the Notion of Religion and of a {{smallcaps|God}} from the Minds of Men, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:C. Lamb Glenarvon|volume=I|chapter=VIII|page=69|passage=[I]t seemed his desire to '''raze''' every trace of sorrow from the memory of his child; and to conceal the ravages of death under the appearance at least of wild and unceasing gaiety.}}
## {{lb|en|also|figurative}} To [[erase#Verb|erase]] (a [[record#Noun|record]], [[text#Noun|text]], etc.), originally by [[scrape#Verb|scraping]]; to [[rub out]], to [[scratch out]].
##* {{RQ:Skelton Poetical Works|volume=I|poem=Garland|lines=1478–1480|page=420|passage=Suppleyng to Fame, I besought her grace, / And that it wolde please her, full tenderly I prayd, / Owt of her bokis {{w|Apollo}} to '''rase'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Wyatt Poems|poem=Sonnet 31|stanza=1|lines=13–14|page=43|passage=The wound alas happe in some other place, / From whence no toole away the skar can '''race'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Turberville Epitaphes|poem=Take Time|folio=32|verso=1|folioend=33|folioref=33|passage=[D]rowſie drouping Age, / incroaching on apace, / With penſiue Plough will '''raze''' your hue / and Beauties beames deface.}}
##* {{RQ:Spenser Amoretti|title=GWI|sig=&#91;¶4&#93;|page=8|passage=[N]o malice of ſucceeding daies, / can '''raſe''' thoſe records of thy laſting praiſe.}}
##* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|year=1596.2|book=V|canto=IX|stanza=26|page=303|passage=Thus there he ſtood, vvhyleſt high ouer his head, / There vvritten vvas the purport of his ſin, / In cyphers ſtrange, that fevv could rightly read, / BON FONS: but ''bon'' that once had vvritten bin, / VVas '''raced''' out, and ''Mal'' vvas novv put in.}}
##* {{RQ:Heywood Fair Maid|page=38|passage=''Goodl''{{quote-gloss|''ack''}}. My name is Captaine ''Thomas Good——'' / ''Beſſ''{{quote-gloss|''e Bridges''}}. I can ſee no good in thee, '''Race''' that ſyllable / Out of thy name.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Sonnets|sonnet=25|sig=C2|page=43|passage=The painefull vvarrier famoſed for vvorth, / After a thouſand victories once foild, / Is from the booke of honour '''raſed''' quite, / And all the reſt forgot for vvhich he toild: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Of Reformation|year=1916|page=23|passage=[O]f those Books that passe for authentick who knows what hath bin tamper'd withall, what hath bin '''raz'd''' out, what hath bin inserted, {{...}}}}
